{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Security review criteria and Pass 2 scoring audit\n\nDate: 2026-08-28\n\n## Scope\n\n- Compared `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-security.md` with the agentic-dev-platform `security-review/v1` prompt and its runtime wrapper.\n- Inventoried Rebar's security detector manifests, code-review routing, Pass 2 verification contract, Pass 3 validity and impact arithmetic, adjacent overlays, and plan-review T5c security criterion.\n- Ran two independent background challenge passes. One disputed claimed finding-generation gaps against the existing criteria. The other evaluated minimal changes to the generic validity and impact mechanism.\n\n## Prompt comparison\n\n- Rebar's overlay is narrower in declared recall but much more specific about eight concerns, false-positive controls, repository grounding, scanner ownership, evidence shape, and four-pass responsibility.\n- The project prompt adds residual `secret-handling`, residual `other OWASP-class weaknesses`, and explicit exploitability and change-causation requirements for blocking.\n- The project invokes its own one-sentence prompt through Rebar's generic runner. It does not invoke `code-review-security.md` or inherit Rebar's four-pass security rubric.\n- The project labels the subject as a code diff, but the draft workflow supplies branch, commit, filenames, and an optional implementation summary rather than patch hunks.\n\n## Criteria audit\n\n- Explicit code-review gaps remain for runtime secret egress and persistence, sensitive output, authentication and session lifecycle, security logging and monitoring, selected browser and HTTP protections, and deterministic CVE intelligence.\n- Resource abuse is not a Rebar-wide omission because the performance overlay can cover it.\n- Dependency vulnerabilities have partial coverage through the supply-chain overlay, but no deterministic advisory-database scanner.\n- SSRF, redirects, and header attacks may be generalized from taint-to-sink reasoning. Their current recall contract is under-specified rather than absent.\n- Plan-review T5c already covers lower-trust actors, authentication, encryption, least privilege, and secret lifecycle. The remaining concern is implementation-time recall.\n- Gitleaks findings are post-filtered to changed-file locations. A changed route that exposes an unchanged secret-bearing file can fall between the detector and the overlay's secret-delegation rule.\n- The security eval spec contains inconsistent ownership. One case expects an LLM hardcoded-secret finding while another delegates secret leakage to the deterministic criterion.\n\n## Confirmed Pass 2 defects\n\n- `path_reachable` is averaged with other validity answers rather than treated as foundational. Six `yes` answers and `path_reachable=no` yield validity `0.8571`. A visible, common serious security bypass then has impact `0.72` and priority `0.6171`, above the security threshold `0.54`. The background reviewer executed this case and confirmed a BLOCK.\n- `impact_code` derives production impact from four consequence booleans. A finding with `prod_impact=high` and none of those booleans receives impact zero. This leaves demonstrated protected-data disclosure and serious core-service unavailability without natural production consequences.\n- Rebar already asks about entailment, reachability, necessary harm, alternative explanations, and mitigation. The gap is their non-foundational arithmetic and caller-focused reachability wording, not the total absence of exploitability reasoning.\n\n## Minimal recommendation\n\n- Add a code-review-specific `validity_code()` through a `validity_fn` seam. Leave plan review unchanged.\n- Set validity to zero when `is_verifiable`, `evidence_entails_finding`, applicable `path_reachable`, or applicable `trigger_available` is definitely `no`.\n- Cap validity at `0.5` when a foundational answer is `insufficient`, or when necessary harm or mitigation absence is `no` or `insufficient`. This preserves defense-in-depth advisories while preventing blocks under the current impact ceiling and thresholds.\n- Keep `path_reachable` narrow and factual. It should ask whether a concrete production caller reaches the path.\n- Add one generic `trigger_available` answer with `yes|no|insufficient|na`. It should ask whether the actor, input source, event, or environment named in the finding can provide the required input or state before the defect occurs.\n- Add `protected_data_disclosed` and `core_capability_unavailable` as serious production booleans at `0.9`, both defaulting false.\n- Broaden `security_bypass_not_enforced_elsewhere` to include absent, bypassed, or ineffective required security checks that let a lower-trust actor perform a protected operation.\n- Do not add separate fields for lower-trust reachability, attacker control, holistic exploit preconditions, security effect, mitigation absence, CIA categories, privilege gain, session compromise, or secret exposure. Those proposals duplicate existing questions, ask broad synthesis questions, or collapse into the two proposed consequences.\n- Fold change causation into evidence entailment initially. Add `change_materially_contributes` only if recorded reviews show pre-existing findings receiving blocks.\n\n## Migration and verification\n\n- Additive contract fields should abstain safely for older payloads.\n- Bump the code-review impact model from `code-v5` to `code-v6`.\n- Do not pool `code-v5` and `code-v6` calibration data.\n- Replay and reapprove thresholds under ADR 0036.\n- Test definite and insufficient foundational answers, structural `na`, both new consequences, MAX behavior, old payload compatibility, the validity-cap invariant, and `code-v6` stamping.\n- The background Pass 2 reviewer ran the focused existing suite and reported 90 passing tests before any changes.\n\n## Outcome\n\nNo repository files were changed. This session produced an evidence-backed criteria assessment and a minimal scoring design for future Rebar work."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787976643260397661,"uuid":"d02994a8-f809-48d8-bb3e-4cd1a4d47514"}